{"id": "43ca7458-588f-4606-a00a-cf1433a5b34a", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:02:42.672821", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "537ae72d-92db-4daf-a8cc-c2991d5d3cbc", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: the environment is completely empty aside from task.json. No network (pip download fails), no numpy/torch, no GPU, Python 3.14. So this is a purely design-only task, and the InfLLM structure has to be summarized from clearly labeled general knowledge (InfLLM paper: Zhang et al. 2024, \"LLM Maybe LongLM: Self-Extend LLM Context Window Without Tuning\" — actually InfLLM is from that paper; there's also a separate \"InfLLM: Unveiling Infinite Length Ability in LLM\" by Wang et al. 2024 as an independent memory-unit-based method). Need to be careful: two works exist:\n\n1. \"LLM Maybe LongLM: Self-Extend LLM Context Window Without Tuning\" (Zhang et al., 2024, ICML) — introduces InfLLM, which organizes context into blocks (\"memory units\") of size d=128, uses the mean of hidden states as the memory content representation and the last token of the block as the retrieval key representation; in the retrieval state the model attends over the most recent memory units plus top-k retrieved units via similarity q·k (retrieval key = projection of the last-token hidden state). This is the open-source one at github.com/OpenBMB/InfLLM.\n\n2. \"InfLLM: Unveiling the Infinite Length Ability of LLM\" (Wang et al., 2024, ICLR 2025?) — memory-unit-based, with a conventional memory unit and a memory retriever with memory units organized into hierarchical memory pools; trains an independent retrieval model.\n\nThe general-knowledge summary should be clearly labeled, and the two variants should be mentioned. Task's components:\n\nDynamic context memory lookup: retrieval state, similarity between query vector (current hidden state of the query token / projection of the last-layer embedding) and memory unit keys (hidden state of the last token of the unit), top-k selection, which units are retrievable (all non-recent past units), attention restricted to selected units + recent contiguous units, k, memory window size.\n\nUnit representation: unit/block size (e.g., 128 tokens), key representation (last-token hidden state vs. mean pooling vs. independent encoder), content representation (token-level hidden states vs. pooled), positional handling within retrieved blocks (InfLLM reassigns positions — uses relative-position re-allocation: assigns the same positional offset as recent memory to retrieved blocks), and whether representations come from the LM's own hidden states (frozen, no tuning) vs. an independent trained encoder.\n\nCandidate framings:\n\nA. Ablation ladder (component removal / perturbation): replace dynamic retrieval with fixed-stride blocks, random blocks, last-k only, oracle retrieval (blocks containing gold evidence), vary k, vary block size. What we can tell: necessity / sensitivity of each lookup component; what we cannot tell: interaction effects / representation-lookup confounds unless crossed.\n\nB. Budget-matched representation swap: keep the retrieval pipeline fixed, swap out unit representations (last-token key vs. mean-pool key vs. key from an independent encoder; block size 32/128/512), measure retrieval hit rate (block recall of evidence) and downstream NIAH/LongBench scores at the same token budget. What we can tell: causal effect of representation choice on both retrieval quality and end task; what we cannot tell: what we cannot separate is… we can't tell whether lookup dynamics matter, since those are held fixed.\n\nC. Diagnostic / transfer analysis: measure block-level retrieval recall against evidence localization across task families and lengths, regress downstream performance on retrieval recall; plus oracle upper bounds and random lower bounds to bound the headroom attributable to lookup. What we can tell: how much of the variance in the end metric is attributable to lookup failure; what we cannot tell: strict causal claims, compensation via attention within blocks.\n\nD. 2×2 factorial design: {dynamic top-k vs. fixed/random/oracle} × {LM-hidden-state units vs. swapped representation}, budget-matched. This is essentially a combination of A and B.\n\nAssumptions / unknowns: model scale (official InfLLM uses Llama-2-7B-32K/YaRN, and some are 13B/70B), whether we use the official OpenBMB/InfLLM code against an internal reimplementation, task families (synthetic NIAH / variable-length retrieval; LongBench subset; passkey / number retrieval; possible QA), budget: since this environment has no GPU, no network, no torch/numpy, this has to remain a design document with a feasibility argument; assume the compute budget stated in the design (e.g., 7B model, ~A100-80GB GPU-hours), number of seeds, whether training is allowed (InfLLM is tuning-free; the independent-encoder variant may require training → avoid, or use a frozen off-the-shelf retriever like Contriever/bge).\n\nNow, submit the stage=plan handoff along with the investigation report, structure summary, framings, and assumptions. The required handoff stage is plan, so I'll do that now.\n\nEnvironment investigation is complete. The workspace is effectively empty — task.json only, no code, no data, no ML stack, no GPU, no network. So the structural summary below is explicitly based on general knowledge about InfLLM. I'm reporting that along with candidate framings and open questions.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "RECONNAISSANCE RESULTS (design-only; nothing executed as an experiment)\n\n1) WHAT IS ACTUALLY PRESENT IN /workspace\n- /workspace contains exactly one file: task.json (the task question JSON; it states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\").\n- No InfLLM code, no fork/checkout of OpenBMB/InfLLM, no config files, no similarity/top-k/retrieved-memory implementation, no memory-unit or block-size parameters, no context-memory window code.\n- No benchmark harnesses (no LongBench, no needle-in-a-haystack generators, no retrieval/length-generalization eval scripts) and no dataset manifests or task lists. /home/actor and /tmp are empty. A filesystem scan for 'infllm'/'longbench' found nothing.\n- Software environment: system Python 3.14.4 with only OS/system packages (ansible, apt, etc.). No torch, no transformers, no numpy (verified imports fail). pip cannot fetch packages (network disabled — pip download fails). nvidia-smi fails: no GPU. 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM.\n- Plain conclusion: the repository is EMPTY with respect to the task. Everything below in item (2) is therefore general knowledge, clearly labeled as such, not repo observation.\n\n2) HOW INFLLM IS STRUCTURED (labeled GENERAL KNOWLEDGE, not repo-derived)\nTwo related works share the name; the open-source one most people mean is Zhang et al. 2024, \"LLM Maybe LongLM: Self-Extend LLM Context Window Without Tuning\" (OpenBMB/InfLLM). A second work, Wang et al. 2024 \"InfLLM: Unveiling the Infinite Length Ability of LLM\", uses a separately trained memory retriever and hierarchical memory pools. The design should pin down which one is in scope. For the OpenBMB variant:\n- Context is segmented into fixed-size blocks (\"memory units\", d=128 tokens in the released config). No tuning of the LM is required.\n- Dynamic context memory lookup components: (a) retrieval KEY per unit = hidden state of the LAST token of the block (in the current layer); (b) memory CONTENT per unit = the full block of token hidden states; (c) QUERY = current token's hidden state, compared to unit keys via a similarity computation (scaled dot-product-style scoring on the key vectors); (d) TOP-K selection over all non-recent past units (released config k≈4 per layer group); (e) attention state: a token attends to (i) its local window, (ii) the K most recent contiguous units (\"context memory\"), and (iii) the dynamically retrieved top-k units — i.e., retrieval state vs. memory state per layer; (f) positional treatment: retrieved blocks are re-indexed so each retrieved unit is assigned positions continuing from the recent-context offset (blocks appended in retrieval order), which is itself a design choice.\n- Knobs that count as \"unit representations\": unit/block granularity (32/128/512); key representation (last-token hidden state vs. mean-pooled hidden state vs. max/CLS-style summary vs. a separate frozen text encoder such as Contriever/bge — the latter only if we deliberately break the \"no separate encoder\" property of the original); content granularity (token-level hidden states vs. pooled unit content fed to attention); positional reassignment scheme for retrieved units; which layer's hidden states serve as keys; whether keys are recomputed per layer or shared.\n- The two axes in the question map cleanly: \"dynamic context memory lookup\" = (c)+(d)+(e) plus k and window size; \"unit representations\" = the granularity/key/content/positionality knobs.\n\n3) CANDIDATE FRAMINGS (not committed)\nA. Ablation ladder on the lookup axis. Keep unit representations fixed (original last-token-key, 128-token blocks); step through: full InfLLM → oracle retrieval (blocks known to contain evidence) → random k blocks → fixed-stride k blocks → recent-only (no retrieval) → full attention upper bound. Rationale: cleanly ranks how much end-task score depends on the retrieval being dynamic vs. merely block-sparse. Concludes: causal necessity of dynamic selection and of k; does NOT isolate representation choice, and oracle needs evidence labels (easy for synthetic NIAH, awkward for LongBench QA).\nB. Matched-budget representation swap. Keep the lookup pipeline fixed; swap unit key/content representations (last-token vs. mean-pool vs. separate frozen encoder) and block sizes (32/128/512) at equal attended-token budget; measure BOTH block-level evidence recall (retrieval hit-rate) and downstream score. Rationale: separates \"what a unit looks like\" from \"how units are chosen\", with recall as the mediator. Concludes: causal effect of representation choice and where it acts (retrieval accuracy vs. in-block reading); does NOT by itself speak to whether dynamic lookup matters (it is held fixed).\nC. Diagnostic/transfer analysis. Instrument InfLLM as-is: log per-query retrieved-block hits vs. gold evidence spans across lengths (8k–128k) and task families; regress downstream degradation on retrieval recall; bracket each task with random-block floor and oracle ceiling. Rationale: cheapest, quantifies how much of InfLLM's variance is explained by lookup failures without any retraining. Concludes: correlational attribution and headroom bounds; does NOT establish causality (the LM may partially compensate for bad retrieval inside attention), and gold-evidence localization is imperfect on reasoning/multi-hop tasks.\n- Note: A 2×2 factorial (oracle/random/dynamic × representation variants) is the natural upgrade if budget allows, since A and B each leave the other axis confounded; worth deciding only after the researcher fixes the compute budget.\n\n4) CONSEQUENTIAL ASSUMPTIONS AND UNKNOWNS\n- Scope of \"InfLLM\": assume the OpenBMB tuning-free variant (Llama-2-7B-32K / YaRN-based, per the released repo) unless the researcher says the Wang et al. retriever-trained variant is intended — this changes the design substantially (their retriever is trainable; ours can only swap in frozen encoders).\n- Model scale/variants: assume 7B-class single model, possibly one 13B replication point; 30B+/multi-node is assumed out of scope. NEEDS CONFIRMATION.\n- \"Long-context tasks\": assume three families — synthetic retrieval (passkey/number/NIAH with known evidence spans), LongBench-style subsets requiring localization (e.g., HotpotQA-style multi-hop, traversal), and length-generalization evals (train-window length vs. eval length). Whether LongBench's summarization/code subsets are in scope is an open question (gold spans unavailable there, hurting framings A and C).\n- Compute: no GPU and no network in this environment; torch/transformers are not even installed, so NOTHING can be run here. The deliverable must be a pure design document with feasibility argued against an external budget. I assume a nominal budget to size the grid (e.g., on the order of 1–2k A100-80GB-hours, 3 seeds on synthetic tasks, 1 seed on LongBench) — this is an assumption, not a given.\n- Implementation source: assume we may clone official OpenBMB/InfLLM + LongBench externally; no such code exists here to modify.\n- Separate-encoder variants: assume frozen off-the-shelf retrievers only (no training), since training a memory retriever is likely beyond an ablation-budget and changes InfLLM's tuning-free premise.\n- Oracle definitions: assume synthetic tasks provide gold blocks; for organic QA we would need a span-annotation step — feasibility TBD.\n- Deliverable form: pure design document (protocol, arms, metrics, budget table), no executed results, per task.json (\"repositories and compute are not required\") and the instruction not to claim experiments were run.\n\nRECOMMENDED NEXT STEP: researcher to confirm (i) which InfLLM variant, (ii) task-family list, (iii) nominal GPU-hour budget; then I will develop the full design, most likely framing B as the backbone with A's oracle/random brackets folded in as ceiling/floor arms (a partial factorial), unless budget forces a single-axis choice.", "evidence": "Repo inspection (this session): /workspace contains only task.json (contents confirm \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"); /home/actor and /tmp empty; filesystem scan for infllm/longbench found no matches; python3 imports of torch/transformers/numpy all fail (ModuleNotFoundError); pip download fails (network disabled); nvidia-smi fails (no GPU driver); python 3.14.4, 224 CPUs, ~2TB RAM. Environment spec in task also states gpu_enabled=false, datasets=[], dependencies not preinstalled. St