{"id": "5a769551-4cbe-4a64-ba9d-027bcd7d5815", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:03:18.907000", "source": "user", "parent_id": "ee0d5afa-edb9-446b-9b07-e8d5ca804649", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Reconnaissance is solid and the empty-repo finding is accepted as stated — I'm treating all of section (2) as general knowledge, not repo evidence, and that labeling must carry into the design document. Your three framings are well-scoped and the partial-factorial upgrade is the right instinct. I'm approving the approach; please now produce the full design document. Decisions on your open items, stated as my directives (they fix scope):\\n\\n(i) VARIANT: Primary = OpenBMB tuning-free InfLLM (Zhang et al. 2024). Treat the Wang et al. trained-retriever variant OUT of scope for arms, but include a short paragraph in the design noting which conclusions would not transfer to it (since its retriever is trainable, \\\"representation choice\\\" there is confounded with training). Keep all representation swaps frozen/no-training, as you proposed.\\n\\n(ii) TASK FAMILIES: Accept your three families, with these constraints. (a) Synthetic retrieval (passkey / number-retrieval / multineedle NIAH) — primary, because gold blocks are exact; this is where oracle and random-bracket arms live. (b) LongBench-style subsets — restrict to localization-amenable ones (e.g. HotpotQA/Durender-style multi-hop QA, traversal, duplicate/passage-retrieval style); explicitly EXCLUDE summarization/code/synthetic-code subsets and state why (no gold evidence span ⇒ oracle undefined), rather than inventing an annotation pipeline as if it were cheap. If you want one organic oracle arm, scope it to a small hand-annotated subset with an inter-annotator-agreement control, and mark it as the highest-risk item. (c) Length-generalization sweep as an analysis axis (eval length 8k→128k), not a separate task family.\\n\\n(iii) BUDGET: I am authorizing you to size against a nominal external budget on the order of 1–2k A100-80GB-hours, 3 seeds synthetic / 1 seed organic, single 7B-class model, with one 13B point as a clearly optional replication. Label these as design assumptions wherever used, and include an explicit \\\"if budget is halved, cut these arms first\\\" degradation note so the sizing failure mode is visible.\\n\\nBackbone: framing B (matched-budget representation swap) with framing A's oracle/random/fixed-stride/recent-only/full-attention brackets folded in, as you recommended. Requirements the design must satisfy:\\n\\n1. Explicit matched-budget statement. Every arm must attend to the same number of tokens/blocks per layer; show the budget arithmetic for block sizes 32/128/512 so the reader can verify \\\"equal budget\\\" is actually equal, including how the local window and K-most-recent units are held constant.\\n\\n2. Confound control you flagged yourself. Block-size changes co-vary with positional-reassignment behavior and with per-block key quality. Add a control arm (or a crossed cell) that separates these: e.g. 128-token blocks scored as 32-token sub-unit keys, or constant position scheme vs. original re-indexing at fixed block size. Say plainly which cell of the grid identifies each effect and what remains unidentifiable.\\n\\n3. Mediator measured, not assumed. Block-level evidence recall (retrieval hit-rate vs. gold blocks) must be reported as a distinct outcome from downstream score, for the same runs, so the claim \\\"representation acts through retrieval accuracy\\\" is testable rather than asserted. Also specify the mediation-style comparison you would run (recall vs. score at matched recall, e.g. the oracle/random arms give you points at fixed retrieval accuracy).\\n\\n4. Lookup-axis knobs handled, not just the representation axis. Since the question asks how critical dynamic lookup is, k and the local-window/recent-unit sizes need at least a coarse sensitivity grid, and you should state whether the k-sensitivity is a full factorial with representations or a one-at-a-time probe (my preference: one-at-a-time, and say so, to protect budget).\\n\\n5. Statistics and power. For each comparison, state the test, unit of replication, and seed/variance handling (3 seeds synthetic, 1 seed organic is thin — acknowledge the organic results are descriptive and cannot support confident interaction claims, and avoid designing any headline interaction that depends on n=1).\\n\\n6. Feasibility section. Compute table per arm (runs × sequence length × approx GPU-hours), the total, and the cut-first list. State clearly nothing was executed here (no GPU, no torch, no network, empty repo) and that all numeric feasibility figures are estimates from stated assumptions, not measurements.\\n\\n7. Success/failure criteria up front. Write down in advance what pattern of results would license each of these three conclusions: \\\"dynamic lookup is critical\\\", \\\"dynamic lookup contributes little beyond block-sparsity/locality\\\", \\\"unit representation choice is critical\\\", \\\"representation choice is largely immaterial at matched budget\\\" — including what would count as evidence of LM-internal compensation for poor retrieval (the confound you raised against framing C).\\n\\nDeliver the design as one document: rationale, arm table, metrics, analysis plan, confounds and threats-to-validity, feasibility/budget, and a limitations section. Do not present any result, number, or qualitative trend as a finding.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "2b42c808-1cfc-4f9b-ac49-05c9997fb55b", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:04:35.366176", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "5a769551-4cbe-4a64-ba9d-027bcd7d5815", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The researcher has approved and is asking for a complete design document at stage=result. All 7 requirements need to be satisfied. Let me carefully think through the design content, especially the budget calculations, arm table, statistics, and feasibility figures.\n\nKey design elements:\n\nModel: Llama-2-7B-32K style (or a modern equivalent model with base RoPE, e.g., 7B model + YaRN scaling as the original InfLLM does). Single model; 13B is optional.\n\nMemory configuration held constant across all arms:\n- Local window W = 2048 tokens (as in the original InfLLM retrieval state... actually the original uses block size 128, k=4, and a \"most recent K blocks\" context; let's define our own). What we fix: local window W=1024 or 2048; number of most-recent context units; number of retrieved units k.\n\nMatching the budget: attended tokens per query token per layer = local window W + (n_recent + k) × block size B. Hmm, but when the block size changes, if we keep n_recent and k constant as block counts, the token budget grows with B: e.g., B=32: recent 8 blocks = 256 tokens; k=4 → 128. B=128: recent 8 = 1024, k=4 → 512. B=512: 4096 + 2048. This is not equal. To equalize: fix the total token budget for retrieved + recent context, C_ctx = 1536 tokens (e.g., 1024 recent + 512 retrieved), and set n_recent = floor(1024/B), k = floor(512/B). B=32: n_recent=32, k=16; B=128: n_recent=8, k=4; B=512: n_recent=2, k=1. This keeps attended tokens per layer identical: W + 1536. Also, the original InfLLM default B=128 with n_recent=8, k=4 matches exactly — a good anchor point. Local window W held constant at 2048 tokens across all arms (window is on the token side, so independent of B).\n\nBut with B=512, k=1 gives only one retrieved block — the dynamic-retrieval arm at B=512 has very little retrieval capacity; note this as an inherent tension: under a matched budget, large blocks reduce the number of retrievable units, and this is exactly the granularity trade-off that the study aims to quantify. Alternative: include one B=512 \"budget-relaxed\" diagnostic cell (k=4 → 2048+... tokens) as well, and explicitly flag it as budget-unmatched. Good to include as a diagnostic rather than a headline.\n\nRepresentation swap (Axis R):\n- R1: Last-token hidden-state key (InfLLM original), token-level content, original re-indexing.\n- R2: Mean-pooled hidden-state key over the whole block, token-level content, original re-indexing.\n- R3: External frozen encoder key (e.g., Contriever or bge-small) computed per block; content is still the LM's token hidden states; re-indexing is original. This swaps out only the key space; attention scores for retrieved content — wait, in InfLLM attention over retrieved blocks uses the LM's normal attention over those tokens, not key-space similarity. So swapping out the key affects retrieval only. Good.\n- R4 (optional/deprioritized): Pooled content (attend to a single summary token per block rather than all tokens) — tests \"content granularity.\" Hmm, does this change the token budget? If content is pooled to 1 token per unit but we keep the attended-token count the same... actually, pooled content reduces compute; to match budget, keep B tokens of attention and have them be replicated? More simply: R4 attends to n units each represented by 1 pooled token + the same number of local tokens? This changes the semantics of the budget. R4 should perhaps be listed as a supplementary arm under a \"matched unit count, not matched token\" budget — mark as optional. Or define matched budget as \"tokens attended\" and have pooled content attend to (n_recent+k)×B \"pseudo\"... no, wrong. Better: include pooled content as a clearly labeled token-budget-unmatched diagnostic (cheaper) and note it in the threat section. Or drop it to protect budget. Include it as an optional cut-first-tier arm.\n\nPosition control (requirement 2):\n- P1: Original re-indexing (assign positions to retrieved units continuing from the recent-context offset, in retrieval order).\n- P2: Constant/original-position scheme: assign tokens of retrieved blocks their original absolute positions (with NTK/YaRN handling long distances). P2 may degrade due to position extrapolation, but is feasible with YaRN.\nCross R1 × {P1, P2} at fixed B=128: isolates the position scheme at fixed block size. Then B ∈ {32,128,512} under R1 at fixed budget. And a crossed sub-unit control: score 32-token sub-unit keys but read out the containing 128-token block? The researcher suggested \"score 128-token blocks as 32-token sub-unit keys\" — i.e., retrieval granularity 32 / readout granularity 128 (retrieved block = 4 consecutive sub-units). This decouples key granularity from readout/position granularity. Cells:\n  - X1: retrieval granularity 32 / readout 128 (4-consecutive-concatenation) vs. R1@B=32 (both 32) vs. R1@B=128 (both 128). This triangle identifies: the difference B=32 vs. B=128 = total granularity effect; X1 vs. R1@128 = pure key-granularity effect (readout/position identical); X1 vs. R1@32 = pure readout/position effect (keys identical). Residual interaction (key×readout interaction) remains unidentifiable without a fourth cell — actually, with a full 2×2 {key granularity 32/128}×{readout 32/128} it becomes identifiable. X1 gives key32/read128, and we need key128/read32? That would be: score the block by last-token at 128 but read out only the containing 32-token sub-block — weird/ill-defined. Simpler: state that the 2×2 requires key128/read32, that this is semantically ill-defined (a 128-token key pointing at 32 tokens of content requires a block→sub-block mapping rule), so we leave the interaction unidentifiable and claim additivity with a caveat. A good, honest description.\n  Budget for X1: retrieval granularity 32, readout unit 128. Keep token budget: retrieved content tokens = k×128 with k=4 → same as R1@128. Keys are 4× more numerous (more candidates to scan) — retrieval cost changes but attended tokens are the same. Fine — \"budget\" refers to attention/reading budget; scan cost is noted separately.\n\nLookup arms (Axis L, at best configuration of R1@B=128... actually anchor at R1@B=128):\n- L1: Full InfLLM dynamic top-k (anchor).\n- L2: Oracle top-k (gold-evidence blocks; synthetic tasks only).\n- L3: Random k blocks (seeded; average over 3 random draws? use fixed RNG per run).\n- L4: Fixed-stride k blocks (uniformly spaced).\n- L5: Recent-only (k=0, only local window + recent units; matched? drops the retrieved 512 tokens — to keep matched budget, increase recent units by k: n_recent' = n_recent + k. Good: L5 consumes budget by \"retrieving the wrong blocks\" = the most-recent ones. Good matched-budget control).\n- L6: Full attention (upper bound; budget-unmatched, labeled as upper bound).\n- Optionally L7: shuffle order within retrieval (position-ordering probe) — could be cut.\n\nk sensitivity (requirement 4): one-factor-at-a-time probe around the anchor: k ∈ {1,2,4,8,16} at B=128 (retrieved-token budget 128..2048 varies — this axis deliberately breaks the matched budget to measure the budget-vs-k trade-off; state this explicitly). Also recent-units n_recent ∈ {2,8,16} and local window W ∈ {1024, 2048, 4096} — one at a time. These probe arms are budget-varied by design; note that these answer \"sensitivity to lookup allocation\" rather than \"representation comparison,\" and that comparisons across k are confounded with total attention budget.\n\nTasks:\n(a) Synthetic: passkey (1/3 needles), number retrieval, multi needle (1–8), varying lengths 8k–128k. Exact gold blocks. 3 seeds. Metrics: exact-match accuracy; block recall.\n(b) LongBench-style localization-compatible: HotpotQA (LongBench version), 2wikimqa?, Dureader?, traversal (synthetic from piqa? traversal is synthetic), paste/passage-retrieval (LongBench \"duplicate letter/passage\" are synthetic-style). Note: LongBench has a max of about 65k; if a harness that packs up to 128k is available, extend length by padding to 128k with distractor documents (padding is a standard technique — needle-in-a-haystack padding; state as design intent). Include HotpotQA, 2WikiMultihopQA, Dureader, traversal, passage-retrieval. Exclude: summarization (samsum, multi_news), code, and datasets without answer-evidence-span support — because oracle is undefined. Organic oracle: an optional small hand-annotated subset (e.g., 100 HotpotQA items with evidence passages), 2 annotators + IAA (Cohen's κ ≥ 0.7 gate); highest-risk item.\n(c) Length axis: evaluation at 8k/16k/32k/64k/128k as a sweep within each arm (synthetic is by construction; organic via padding).\n\nNumber of samples: synthetic: e.g., 100 probes per (type × #needles × length)? Need to size. Number of runs per arm: total ~1000 synthetic eval items + ~500 organic eval items. Compute estimate: 7B model, decoding is short-answer (few tokens), prefill dominates: roughly measure prefill FLOPs as 2×P×L... approximation: 1 forward pass at L=64k tokens on 7B ≈ 2×7e9×65536 ≈ 9.2e14 FLOPs ≈ about 0.9 seconds on an A100 at effective ~1e15 FLOPS (realistic effective ~40% of 19.5e13? A100 bf16 = 312 TFLOPS peak; effective ~150 TFLOPS = 1.5e14). Let's be careful: A100-80GB bf16 dense peak ≈ 312 TFLOPS. Effective for long-sequence prefill ≈ 100–150 TFLOPS. Forward at 64k tokens ≈ 9.2e14 FLOPs → ≈ 6–9 seconds on one A100. Sparse attention methods like InfLLM save attention FLOPs but the dominant cost at 7B is usually MLP; MLP cost scales with token count in the prefill pass (all tokens processed) — InfLLM processes all tokens at each layer (key computation) but attention is sparse. So full prefill ≈ same as dense for MLP ≈ 2×7e9×65536×(2/3 for attention portion?) let's keep the rough estimate: ~8 sec/item at 64k, scaling linearly with length. Full attention at 128k is heavier on attention but still ~4× the 32k cost.\n\nBudget table: per arm: synthetic set ~750 items averaged at length ~48k ≈ equivalent of 1 forward pass per item (greedy, ≤32 new tokens) ≈ 6 sec × (48/64)... let me compute with a simpler rate card: assume ~0.15 GPU-hours per 100 items at 64k... let me calculate: at effective 120 TFLOPS, 1 item at 64k ≈ 9.2e14/1.2e14 = 7.7 sec; plus decode 32 tokens × (2×7e9 ≈ 1.4e10 + KV) ≈ negligible, ~1 sec. So at 64k ≈ 9 sec/item. Length mix {8,16,32,64,128}k: mean 49.6k → ~7 sec/item. Synthetic per arm: 5 lengths × (passkey 50 + number 50 + multi-needle 3 needle counts × 50 = 150)... hmm, define: synthetic suite = 600 items (passkey 30, number 30, NIAH multi 40 each for 1/2/4/8 needles per length × 5 lengths = ...). More simply: suite = 500 items, average 49.6k → 500×7 sec ≈ 3500 sec ≈ 1 GPU-hour per seed. 3 seeds ≈ 3 GPU-hours per synthetic arm. Tiny. Organic: HotpotQA 200×avg 40k, etc. ~800 items ≈ 1.6 GPU-hours; full attention arm is more expensive (quadratic attention term): at 128k, attention FLOPs 2×2×L²×n_layers×d... ≈ compute: 4 × L² × H_total... attention prefill FLOPs ≈ 2 × 2 × L² × n_layers × d_model = 4×(65536²)×32×4096 ≈ 4×4.3e9×1.31e5 ≈ 2.25e15 — comparable to/1.7× the MLP term at 64k; at 128k attention dominates (~4×). Full attention at 128k ≈ 8× the 64k cost/item ≈ 60+ sec/item. Still fine. Then why 1–2k GPU-hours? Because of overhead: YaRN setup, retries, key caching for external encoder, multiple runs, 13B replication, oracle arm infrastructure, and the k-sensitivity grid (~15 probe cells). Estimate headline grid at ~400–700 GPU-hours, 13B replication +150–250, probes +150, totaling ~900–1200, within budget with a 30% buffer. Honest about this being an estimate.\n\nArm count:\nHeadline grid (matched budget):\n- Representation axis at B=128: R1 (anchor), R2 (mean-pool key), R3 (external frozen encoder key) = 3 arms.\n- Block size: R1@B32, R1@B512 (B128 = anchor) = +2.\n- Position control: R1@B128-P2 = +1.\n- Cross-granularity: X1 (key32/read128) = +1.\nLookup brackets at R1@B128: oracle, random, fixed-stride, recent-only (k→0 with budget reassigned to recent) = +4. Full attention upper bound = +1 (budget-unmatched, labeled).\nHeadline total = 12 arms (11 matched + 1 upper bound). Plus optional pooled content R4 (cut-first) and optional hand-annotated oracle for organic.\n\nProbes (one factor at a time, anchor config): k ∈ {1,2,8,16} (+4 cells), n_recent ∈ {2,16} (+2), W ∈ {1024,4096} (+2) = 8 probe cells.\n\nStatistics (requirement 5):\n- Unit of replication: evaluation item (primary), model seed (secondary). Synthetic accuracy compared per item across seeds with McNemar's paired exact test / binary item outcomes paired across seeds → use item-mean difference with cluster bootstrap by item; or per-seed 2×2 with McNemar + Stouffer. Simpler specification: primary = per-item paired bootstrap (10k resamples) on accuracy differences, with per-seed results reported; non-parametric.\n- Mediation (requirement 3): report block recall (fraction of gold-evidence blocks included in retrieved set; local window + recent treated as hits) alongside downstream score for every run. Mediation-style comparison: (i) regression of per-item score on retrieval-hit indicator (logistic, item-clustered SE); (ii) compare representation arms to random/oracle arms at matched recall bins — that is, stratify items by achieved recall level and compare R-arm vs. anchor score within strata: if score differences between representations vanish at matched recall → representation works through retrieval accuracy; if differences persist at matched recall → a direct effect (content/position quality) beyond retrieval. This uses oracle/random as calibration points at fixed retrieval accuracy. Good.\n- Detection of LM-internal compensation (requirement 7, compensation against framing C): if R-arm shows score >> random arm despite block recall ≈ random arm (i.e., points close to score but with low recall... hmm. Compensation = high downstream score even with poor retrieval — e.g., score near anchor at recall near random). Actually, compensation means good score despite bad retrieval, via local-window coverage or answer priors. Diagnostic: compare score-vs-recall curves across arms; an arm sitting above the oracle/random interpolation line at matched recall indicates compensation (or miscalibrated gold blocks). Also, control on recent-only arm: if recent-only matches dynamic on a task, dynamic isn't needed.\n- Pre-registered decision rules (requirement 7):\n  * \"Dynamic lookup is important\": dynamic L1 >> {random L3, fixed-stride L4} by Δ ≥ 5 absolute points (synthetic, averaged over seeds, CI excludes 0 at each length ≥32k), AND dynamic is within 5 points of oracle L2, AND the gap grows with length.\n  * \"Dynamic lookup adds little beyond block-sparse locality\": L1 ≈ L3/L4/L5 within 2 points and all well below oracle → retrieval selection unimportant (gold mostly... note: if L1≈random and both fall far short of oracle, selection fails = lookup is important but InfLLM's lookup fails; distinguish: \"little beyond block-sparse locality\" is when L1≈L5 recent-only ≈... hmm. Actually \"little beyond block-sparse locality\" is L1≈L4 fixed-stride≈L5, oracle≫, on tasks where locality is informative. Careful wording: on synthetic where the needle is uniformly placed, locality is uninformative, so random≈recent-only≈low and L1 high ⇒ lookup important. If L1 ≈ random ≈ low with oracle high: dynamic lookup as implemented fails ⇒ the lookup mechanism is important but InfLLM's realization is inadequate — this is a distinct third pattern. Include it.\n  * \"Unit representation choice is important\": spread among R1/R2/R3 (matched budget) ≥ 5 points on the primary suite, CI excluding 0, with recall differences explaining the majority (mediation) or a persistent strata difference.\n  * \"Representation largely irrelevant\": max pairwise difference in the representation triangle ≤ 2 points with CI within ±3 points (TOST-style non-inferiority margin).\n- Organic: single seed → descriptive only; report means with bootstrap CI but pre-commit to no interaction claims; no headline conclusions resting on n=1 seed. Multiple items still give power for main effects; what's missing is seed variance.\n\nAlso state the unit of replication clearly: item is the unit of statistical variation for main effects; seed is the unit for model stochasticity (3 seeds); do not treat runs of the same arm across different lengths as independent for a headline claim (length is an axis; headline averaged over pre-specified length sets).\n\nThreats to validity: block-size confound (addressed by X1 + P2 cell; residual interaction unidentifiable), external-encoder mismatch (R3's key space is unrelated to LM attention → only affects retrieval, as intended; but scan normalization may need temperature calibration — a calibration-fairness threat: tune R3's temperature on a dev split? Top-k doesn't need scores across blocks beyond ranking within the candidate set — ranking within candidates; R3's cosine similarity is monotonic, no calibration issue. But mixing R3 scores with recency? No, InfLLM scores are purely similarity. OK.), YaRN position extrapolation for P2 may unfairly disadvantage P2 — note directional interpretation, gold-block granularity vs. answer span (evidence may straddle blocks), padding artifact in length-sweep for organic, greedy decoding, single model family.\n\nCut-first list (requirement 6): if budget halved: (1) 13B replication, (2) B=512 arm + relaxed diagnostic, (3) pooled content R4 (if included), (4) k-sensitivity to {1,8} only and drop the W/n_recent probe, (5) synthetic NIAH needle-count reduction (8→4), (6) length grid 128k→64k. Absolutely do not cut: anchor, oracle/random brackets, R1/R2/R3 triangle, X1, mediator logging — these carry the core identifiability.\n\nAlso, a general-knowledge labeling note: \"All architectural claims about InfLLM derive from the paper and are labeled as general knowledge; the repo was empty.\"\n\nNow the feasibility numbers. Rate card assumption: A100-80GB, bf16, vLLM or HF batched prefill, effective ~120 TFLOPS per GPU on 7B long-sequence prefill → ~8 sec per item at 64k tokens including short decoding; linear in L (sparse arms; full-attention arm adds quadratic term ~×1.8 at 128k vs. linear extrapolation). Suite sizes: synthetic 500 items/arm ×3 seeds; organic 800 items ×1 seed. Per-arm cost estimate: synthetic: 500 items, length mix {8,16,32,64,128}k mean 49.6k → 500×(8 sec×0.775)=~3100 sec ≈ 0.86 GPU-hours → ×3 ≈ 2.6 GPU-hours. Organic 800 items mean ~40k → 800×5 sec ≈ 1.1 GPU-hours. So per matched arm ≈ ~4 GPU-hours; 12 arms ≈ 50 GPU-hours?? Way below 1–2k. Overhead reality: engineering retries, checkpoints, batch inefficiency (×2), oracle arm overhead, external key encoding (cheap), multi-needle generation, plus full-attention at 128k (maybe 15× anchor at 128k). Even so, ~200–300 GPU-hours. Hmm. Then scale up item counts: use 200 per cell instead of 100 to tighten CIs. Let me reset suite sizes larger: synthetic 1,500 items (3 needle counts × 5 lengths × 100, including passkey/number), organic 1,500. Then per arm: 1500×0.775×8 sec = 9300 sec ≈ 2.6 GPU-hours ×3 = 7.8 + organic 1500×5 sec=2.1 GPU-hours → ~10 GPU-hours per arm. 20 cells ≈ 200 GPU-hours; full attention ~4× ≈ +30; 13B ~2× of everything ≈ +230; total ≈ 450; buffer/overhead ×2 (batch inefficiency, retries, YaNT tuning, temperature calibration dev runs, oracle infra) → ~900–1000. Fits within 1–2k with slack.\n\nAlso note decode: answers are short (≤32 tokens), but at 128k the per-token decode cost with KV cache ~ 2×7e9 FLOPs + memory-bound; ~30 tokens ≈ fine.\n\nAlso, should I run execute_check for something? No need; nothing to compute beyond arithmetic, but I can use python for the feasibility table arithmetic to verify. Good idea — quick arithmetic check.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "python3 - <<'EOF'\n# Feasibility arithmetic check (estimates only)\nL = [8192,16384,32768,65536,131072]\nmeanL = sum(L)/len(L)\n# ~7B model, effective ~120 TFLOPS prefill; fwd flops ~= 2*P*L\nsec64k = 2*7e9*65536/1.2e14\nprint(\"mean eval length (tokens):\", meanL, \"| sec/item @64k:\", sec64k)\nper_item_avg = sec64k*(meanL/65536)\nprint(\"sec/item avg:\", per_item_avg)\n# suites\nsyn_items, org_items = 1500, 1500\nsyn_hours_seed = syn_items*per_item_avg/3600\norg_hours = org_items*per_item_avg*0.8/3600  # organic mean length ~40k\nprint(\"syn GPU-h/seed:\", round(syn_hours_seed,2), \"org GPU-h:\", round(org_hours,2))\nper_arm = 3*syn_hours_seed + org_hours\nprint(\"per matched arm GPU-h:\", round(per_arm,2))\nn_matched = 11; n_probes = 8\nbase = n_matched*per_arm + n_probes*per_arm*0.6  # probes: synthetic suite only\nfa = 4*per_arm\nfa13 = 2*(per_arm)  # 13B replication of anchor+oracle+random+R1/R2/R3 ~6 arms at 2x\ntot = base + fa + fa13\nprint(\"base+probes:\", round(base,1), \"full-attn:\", round(fa,1), \"13B replication:\", round(fa13,1), \"total raw:\", round(tot,1))\nprint(\"with x2 engineering overhead:\", round(2*tot,1))\n# budget arithmetic for matched budget\nW=2048; C_rec=1024; C_ret=512\nfor B in (32,128,512):\n    print(f\"B={B}: n_recent={C_rec//B} k={C_ret//B} attended/layer = {W} + {C_rec} + {C_ret} = {W+C_rec+C_ret}\")\nEOF", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b9af271c8977a916", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-b9af271c8977a916", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"python3 - <<'EOF'\\n# Feasibility arithmetic check (estimates only)\\nL = [8192,16384,32768,65536,131072]\\nmeanL = sum(L)/len(L)\\n# ~7B model, effective ~120 TFLOPS prefill; fwd flops ~= 2*P*L\\nsec64k = 2*7e9*65536/1.2e14\\nprint(\\\"mean eval length (tokens):\\\", meanL, \\\"| sec/item @64k:\\\", sec64k)\\nper_item_avg = sec64k*(meanL/6553