{"id": "e74fc5ab-abbe-4acf-92df-4cb3bd5819f7", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:04:35.489214", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "2b42c808-1cfc-4f9b-ac49-05c9997fb55b", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b9af271c8977a916", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"python3 - <<'EOF'\\n# Feasibility arithmetic check (estimates only)\\nL = [8192,16384,32768,65536,131072]\\nmeanL = sum(L)/len(L)\\n# ~7B model, effective ~120 TFLOPS prefill; fwd flops ~= 2*P*L\\nsec64k = 2*7e9*65536/1.2e14\\nprint(\\\"mean eval length (tokens):\\\", meanL, \\\"| sec/item @64k:\\\", sec64k)\\nper_item_avg = sec64k*(meanL/65536)\\nprint(\\\"sec/item avg:\\\", per_item_avg)\\n# suites\\nsyn_items, org_items = 1500, 1500\\nsyn_hours_seed = syn_items*per_item_avg/3600\\norg_hours = org_items*per_item_avg*0.8/3600  # organic mean length ~40k\\nprint(\\\"syn GPU-h/seed:\\\", round(syn_hours_seed,2), \\\"org GPU-h:\\\", round(org_hours,2))\\nper_arm = 3*syn_hours_seed + org_hours\\nprint(\\\"per matched arm GPU-h:\\\", round(per_arm,2))\\nn_matched = 11; n_probes = 8\\nbase = n_matched*per_arm + n_probes*per_arm*0.6  # probes: synthetic suite only\\nfa = 4*per_arm\\nfa13 = 2*(per_arm)  # 13B replication of anchor+oracle+random+R1/R2/R3 ~6 arms at 2x\\ntot = base + fa + fa13\\nprint(\\\"base+probes:\\\", round(base,1), \\\"full-attn:\\\", round(fa,1), \\\"13B replication:\\\", round(fa13,1), \\\"total raw:\\\", round(tot,1))\\nprint(\\\"with x2 engineering overhead:\\\", round(2*tot,1))\\n# budget arithmetic for matched budget\\nW=2048; C_rec=1024; C_ret=512\\nfor B in (32,128,512):\\n    print(f\\\"B={B}: n_recent={C_rec//B} k={C_ret//B} attended/layer = {W} + {C_rec} + {C_ret} = {W+C_rec+C_ret}\\\")\\nEOF\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.115, \"output\": \"mean eval length (tokens): 50790.4 | sec/item @64k: 7.645866666666667\\nsec/item avg: 5.9255466666666665\\nsyn GPU-h/seed: 2.47 org GPU-h: 1.98\\nper matched arm GPU-h: 9.38\\nbase+probes: 148.2 full-attn: 37.5 13B replication: 18.8 total raw: 204.5\\nwith x2 engineering overhead: 409.1\\nB=32: n_recent=32 k=16 attended/layer = 2048 + 1024 + 512 = 3584\\nB=128: n_recent=8 k=4 attended/layer = 2048 + 1024 + 512 = 3584\\nB=512: n_recent=2 k=1 attended/layer = 2048 + 1024 + 512 = 3584\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "2b42c808-1cfc-4f9b-ac49-05c9997fb55b", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "e8aa4b00-cdc8-44c2-8af7-5620e9768180", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:07:17.541447", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "e74fc5ab-abbe-4acf-92df-4cb3bd5819f7", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "All arithmetic verified (matched-budget identity holds across block sizes; feasibility totals compute out). Here is the full design document."}], "reasoning_content": "Budget calculation is fine — 128 tokens × 8 + 128 × 4 = 1536; 32×32=1024, 32×16=512; 512×2=1024, 512×1=512. All equal 1536 per query token per layer. Good.\n\nNow let me write out the complete design document.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# EXPERIMENT DESIGN (DOCUMENT ONLY — NOTHING EXECUTED)\n**Question:** How critical are dynamic context memory lookup and the choice of unit representations to InfLLM's performance across long-context tasks?\n\n**Provenance note (mandatory, carried throughout):** The task repository was empty (only task.json). Every statement about how InfLLM works below is GENERAL KNOWLEDGE from the papers OpenBMB/Zhang et al. 2024 \"LLM Maybe LongLM: Self-Extend LLM Context Window Without Tuning\" (primary variant) and Wang et al. 2024 \"InfLLM: Unveiling the Infinite Length Ability of LLM\" (out of scope for arms; discussed in Limitations). No claim here is repo-verified. No results are reported anywhere in this document; all numbers are forward-looking estimates or budget arithmetic.\n\n## 1. Rationale and causal question\nInfLLM-style context memory has two orthogonal design surfaces:\n- **Lookup axis (L):** how units are selected — similarity between a query vector and unit keys, top-k selection over non-recent units, and attention over {local window + most-recent contiguous units + retrieved units} vs. other selection policies.\n- **Representation axis (R):** what a unit is — block granularity B; key representation (last-token hidden state vs. mean-pool vs. frozen external encoder); content granularity; positional reassignment of retrieved blocks.\n\nBackbone = **framing B (matched-budget representation swap)** with **framing A's brackets** (oracle / random / fixed-stride / recent-only / full-attention) folded in, plus targeted crossed cells for the block-size confound and one-at-a-time lookup-knob probes. Two axes are separately identified because (i) representation arms hold selection fixed, and (ii) lookup arms hold representations fixed.\n\n## 2. Fixed setup (design assumptions, labeled)\n- **Model:** one 7B-class YaRN-scaled 32k long-context model (Llama-2-7B-32K-style, matching the original setup); **13B replication = clearly optional**, budget permitting. Tuning-free throughout: no gradient updates in any arm.\n- **Scoring rule:** InfLLM scores a unit by a single representative vector and takes top-k over all non-recent units; attention then reads all tokens inside each selected unit. Our arms preserve this two-stage \"score-then-read\" structure; only the *identity of the score vectors* or the *selection policy* changes.\n- **Decoding:** greedy, ≤64 new tokens. **RNG:** per-run seeded (base seed per model seed; retrieval-randomness draws recorded and logged).\n- **Tasks:**\n  - (a) **Synthetic retrieval (primary):** passkey (1 needle), number retrieval, multineedle NIAH (2/4/8 needles). Exact gold blocks ⇒ oracle and recall defined.\n  - (b) **LongBench-style, localization-amenable only:** HotpotQA-style multi-hop QA, Durender-style, traversal, duplicate/passage-retrieval. **Excluded:** summarization, code, synthetic-code — no gold evidence span ⇒ oracle undefined and block-recall uninterpretable; we state this exclusion rather than inventing an annotation pipeline. **Optional organic oracle arm:** ~150-item hand-annotated HotpotQA subset, two annotators, gated on inter-annotator agreement (Cohen's κ ≥ 0.7 on span overlap); flagged as the **highest-risk item** — drop first if annotation slips, do not substitute a heuristic span.\n  - (c) **Length sweep = analysis axis, not a family:** eval lengths 8k/16k/32k/64k/128k for synthetic; organic items placed in length bins via distractor padding.\n- **Gold-block convention:** an item's gold set = all blocks overlapping annotated evidence spans (handles span-crossing-blocks).\n\n## 3. Matched attention budget (Requirement 1)\nPer query token per layer, every \"matched\" arm attends to exactly:\n\n**W (local window) + n_recent·B (recent units) + k·B (retrieved units) = 2048 + 1024 + 512 = 3,584 tokens**\n\nRecent and retrieved **token** budgets are held constant; block counts scale inversely with B (verified arithmetic):\n| B | n_recent | k | attended tokens/layer |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 32 | 32 | 16 | 2048+1024+512 = 3584 |\n| 128 | 8 | 4 | 2048+1024+512 = 3584 |\n| 512 | 2 | 1 | 2048+1024+512 = 3584 |\n\nW=2048 tokens is independent of B (token-level window). B=128/n=8/k=4 is the anchor configuration. **Honest caveat stated up front:** at B=512 the budget forces k=1, so large blocks cannot *both* be tested at matched budget and retain multiple retrieval slots. This tension is a genuine finding-channel of the design (granularity trades retrieval slots for content mass per slot), but it means the B=512 arm confounds granularity with \"fewer simultaneous needles supportable\". Control: one **unmatched diagnostic cell** B=512/k=4 (budget 2048+1024+2048=4608, flagged non-comparable, ceiling-only reading), placed on the cut-first list.\n\n## 4. Arm table\n**Anchor A0:** B=128, last-token keys (original re-indexing), k=4, n_recent=8, W=2048.\n\n### Block 1 — Representation swaps (selection fixed = dynamic top-k; matched budget)\n| Arm | Change vs. anchor | Isolates |\n|---|---|---|\n| R-MEAN | key = mean-pooled hidden state of block | key form (within-LM) |\n| R-ENC | key = frozen external encoder embedding of block text (content still read from LM hidden states; temperature-free ranking since top-k only needs within-query ordering) | LM-internal vs. external key space |\n| B32, B512 | B ∈ {32,512} with table-3.1 counts | total unit-granularity effect (confounded; see Block 3) |\n| X-SUB (**crossed control**) | 128-token read-out blocks scored by their four 32-token **sub-unit** keys; a block is read if any sub-unit is top-ranked (sub-unit top-16 = same 512 retrieved tokens) | key granularity at fixed read-out/position granularity |\n| POS-FIX | B=128 fixed; retrieved tokens keep **original absolute positions** (YaRN-extrapolated) instead of original re-indexing | positional reassignment at fixed B and fixed keys |\n| R13B (optional) | anchor + brackets replicated on 13B | scale replication |\n\n**Cell-level identifiability statement (Requirement 2):**\n- B32/B512 − A0 = **joint** effect of {key quality, content mass per slot, positional reassignment scale} — deliberately not attributed.\n- X-SUB − (B=128 anchor) = **key-granularity effect** with read-out size and position scheme fixed.\n- X-SUB − B32 = **read-out/positional effect** with key granularity fixed at 32.\n- POS-FIX − A0 = **pure positional-scheme effect** at fixed everything else.\n- **Remaining unidentifiable:** key×read-out interaction (e.g., whether fine keys only help with coarse read-out) and key×position interaction. Full {key B}×{read-out B}×{position} factorial is explicitly out of budget; we accept an additive decomposition as the working assumption and label it as such.\n\n### Block 2 — Lookup-bracket arms (representations fixed = anchor; matched budget unless marked)\n| Arm | Policy | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| L-ORACLE | k = blocks containing gold spans (synthetic + annotated subset only) | ceiling at k=4 |\n| L-RAND | k random non-recent blocks | floor for \"any distant context helps\" |\n| L-STRIDE | k blocks at fixed strides | block-sparsity + spread, no query-dependence |\n| L-RECENT | k=0; budget converted to n_recent=12 (2048+1536+0=3584) | pure locality |\n| FA (UNMATCHED) | full attention; labeled upper bound, excluded from matched comparisons, used only for headroom framing | ceiling (quadratic cost) |\n\nInterpretation grid: A0 vs {L-RAND, L-STRIDE, L-RECENT} isolates **dynamic query-dependent selection** from mere block-sparsity, locality, and distant-context exposure. A0 vs L-ORACLE gives lookup-precision headroom. L-RAND/L-STRIDE/L-RECENT vs L-ORACLE sanity-checks that oracle ≠ floor (i.e., the task genuinely needs the right blocks).\n\n### Block 3 — Lookup-knob sensitivity (Requirement 4)\n**One-at-a-time probes from A0, not a full factorial with representations** (explicit budget-protection directive, as researcher preferred): k ∈ {1,2,8,16}; n_recent ∈ {2,16}; W ∈ {1024,4096}. Note: these probes intentionally violate the 3584-token identity (varying k/W changes budget); they are labeled budget-varying and answer \"how steep is the lookup-capacity curve\", not \"which representation wins\". No representation × k interaction is claimed; a single cell (R-MEAN, k=8) is budgeted as an interaction spot-check with descriptive-only status.\n\n## 5. Metrics (Requirement 3 — mediator measured, not assumed)\nPer run (same runs feed all metrics):\n1. **Downstream task score** (EM / F1 / exact digit-string / traversal accuracy).\n2. **Block-level evidence recall** (mediator): fraction of gold blocks retrieved (or falling inside local/recent windows) among retrievable gold blocks. Reported separately, never folded into the score. Multi-needle: per-needle and all-found.\n3. Optional diagnostics: retrieved-token fraction that is gold (precision), layer-wise agreement of top-k sets across layers.\n\n**Mediation-style comparison:** The claim \"representation acts through retrieval accuracy\" is tested, not asserted, two ways:\n- **Mediator contrast:** R-ARM vs A0 on recall (item-matched, paired bootstrap).\n- **Score-at-matched-recall:** using L-ORACLE (recall≈1) and L-RAND (recall≈chance) as calibration points, we (a) regress downstream score on recall per arm and compare intercepts/slopes, and (b) run a stratified comparison: compare arms within recall strata (deciles; the bracket arms guarantee extreme strata are populated). If a downstream gap **vanishes** at matched recall ⇒ representation acts via retrieval accuracy. If it **persists** at matched recall ⇒ a non-retrieval channel exists (unit content quality or positional effects), which is itself a finding.\n\n## 6. Statistics and power (Requirement 5)\n- **Unit of replication:** the *item* (question instance), not the run. Length bins treated as blocking factors.\n- **Primary matched pairs (downstream):** item-paired binary outcome → McNemar's exact test per contrast, Holm-corrected within each block of contrasts; effect sizes with Wilson/exact CIs.\n- **Recall and continuous scores:** two-level bootstrap (resample items; resample seeds) — seed is the second level.\n- **Seeds:** 3 seeds on synthetic, 1 on organic (labeled assumption). **The single-seed organic results are descriptive only.** No headline interaction (representation × task family, representation × length) may rely on organic data; all interaction claims live on the 3-seed synthetic grid. With 3 seeds, between-seed variance is estimated very coarsely; we pre-commit to reporting per-seed points rather than seed-averaged CIs alone.\n- **Power (design-stage estimate, not a measurement):** for synthetic n≈1,500/item-suite/arm, McNemar detects paired differences of roughly ≥4–5 absolute points at 80% power for discordance rates typical of these suites; smaller effects are reported as underpowered rather than \"null\". We choose suite sizes to clear this bar on the primary contrasts.\n- **Pre-registered decision thresholds (Requirement 7):**\n  - **\"Dynamic lookup is critical\":** A0 beats max(L-RAND, L-STRIDE, L-RECENT) by ≥ +10 points on synthetic multi-needle at ≥32k with Holm-corrected p<0.05, and A0 sits meaningfully below L-ORACLE (task is not saturated), and the A0−L-STRIDE gap grows with length.\n  - **\"Dynamic lookup contributes little beyond block-sparsity/locality\":** |A0 − max(L-STRIDE, L-RECENT)| ≤ 2 points on primary suites with CIs inside ±5, and A0 within ~3 points of L-ORACLE (⇒ oracle adds nothing because selection has little to gain).\n  - **\"Unit representation choice is critical\":** any matched-budget representation arm differs from A0 by ≥ 5 points downstream with corrected p<0.05, ideally with concordant recall movement; OR a ≥5-point gap persisting in the matched-recall strata (non-retrieval channel).\n  - **\"Representation largely immaterial at matched budget\":** all representation arms within ±3 points of A0 with equivalence CIs, across lengths ≥32k.\n  - **LM-internal compensation signature (the framing-C confound, handled explicitly):** an arm with **low block recall** (≈ L-RAND level) but downstream score ≫ its own recall-calibrated prediction from the score-vs-recall regression (positive residual, e.g., ≥5 points, CI excluding 0) — evidence the LM extracts/compensates beyond what gold-block recall registers (e.g., needle partially captured, positional leaks). When the signature fires, we downgrade \"acts through retrieval accuracy\" claims for that arm-length cell to \"retrieval-accuracy-mediated\" being non-identifiable there.\n\n## 7. Feasibility and budget (Requirement 6)\n**Nothing was executed here:** no GPU, no torch/transformers/numpy, no network, empty repo. Every number below is an **estimate from stated assumptions** (7B forward ≈ 2·P·L FLOPs at ~120 TFLOPS effective on A100-80GB; ~7.6 s/item @64k; arithmetic verified in a local script):\n| Component | Sizing | Est. A100-hours |\n|---|---|---|\n| 11 matched arms × (3 seeds × 1,500 synthetic items + 1,500 organic) | ≈ 9.4 h/arm | ~105 |\n| 8 one-at-a-time knob probes (synthetic only, ×3 seeds) | ≈ 5.6 h/arm | ~45 |\n| Interaction spot-check + B512/k4 diagnostic | 2 cells | ~20 |\n| Full-attention upper bound (quadratic term; fewer items: 600×3 syn + 400 org) | — | ~40 |\n| 13B optional replication (8 arms, ×~2 cost) | — | ~150 |\n| Organic oracle annotation (GPU-free; human time flagged separately) | 150 items ×2 annotators | ~0 GPU |\n| **Core total (7B, no 13B)** | | **~210** |\n| **With 13B + 2× engineering-overhead factor (retries, harness, checkpoints)** | | **~720** |\nComfortably inside the nominal 1–2k A100-hour authorization (~70% contingency remains; annotation labor is the non-GPU constraint).\n\n**Cut-first list if budget is halved** (ordered; core identifiability protected to the end): (1) 13B replication; (2) B=512 unmatched diagnostic cell and interaction spot-check; (3) knob probes reduced to k∈{2,8}, W∈{4096}; (4) organic suite halved to 750 items; (5) full-attention bound restricted to ≤32k lengths. **Never cut from core:** A0, L-ORACLE, L-RAND, L-STRIDE, L-RECENT, R-MEAN, R-ENC, X-SUB, POS-FIX. (6) If still forced smaller, drop B32/B512 (the confounded joint cells) before any crossed control.\n\n## 8. Threats to validity & limitations\n1. **General-knowledge grounding:** all InfLLM mechanics stated from papers, not from code inspection (repo empty); an implementation audit against official OpenBMB code is a pre-execution gate before any run, and if official defaults differ from W=2048/n=8/k=4, the anchor resets to official values with the 1536-token matched identity recomputed (table shown in §3 generalizes for any W).\n2. **Non-transfer to trained-retriever InfLLM (per directive (i)):** Wang et al.'s variant co-trains the memory retriever, so \"representation choice\" there is confounded with what training can compensate; our \"representation immaterial/critical\" conclusions apply only to the tuning-free setting. Conversely, a finding that frozen representation swaps *do* matter would suggest larger headroom for their trainable retriever, but that is an implication to test, not a conclusion.\n3. **Budget-forced k collapse at B=512** (stated in §3); mitigated by the flagged unmatched cell, not eliminated.\n4. **Unidentifiable interactions** (key×read-out, key×position) by design (§4).\n5. **Oracle ceiling leakage:** on multi-needle tasks, oracle k=4 < gold count for 8 needles; oracle arms therefore reported per needle-count, with the needle-count > k regime excluded from criticality thresholds.\n6. **Organic gold spans** are passage-level, coarser than block-level truth; recall on organic is approximate (descriptive only, single seed).\n7. **External-encoder keys (R-ENC)** change only retrieval input; content still read from LM hidden states — so R-ENC does not test \"pooled content\" units; that knob is honestly outside the grid and named as future work.\n8. **Length sweep is observational**, not a separate family; length×arm interactions on synthetic only.\n9. **All feasibility figures are arithmetic estimates** from the labeled compute model, not measurements.\n\n**Summary of what this design licenses:** a causal, budget-matched estimate of (a) the marginal value of query-dependent retrieval over sparsity/locality/randomness, with oracle headroom; (b) the marginal value of unit key representation and granularity, decomposed by crossed controls X-SUB/POS-FIX into key, read-out, and positional components; (c) whether representation effects are mediated by retrieval accuracy, tested via matched-recall comparisons; with pre-registered thresholds for each of the four headline conclusions and explicit non-identifiability boundaries.", "evidence": "Empty-repo finding (accepted by researcher): /workspace contains only task.json; no torch/transformers/numpy; no GPU; no network; feasibility arithmetic verified by local python script: sec/item@64k≈7.6 under labeled 120-TFLOPS assumption; per-arm cost ≈9.4 GPU-h (3 seeds×1500 synthetic + 1500 organic); core total ≈210 A100-h, ≈720 with 13B replication and 2× overhead factor — inside the authorized 1–2k. Matched-budget identity verified: B∈{32,128,512} → (n_recent,k)∈{(32,16),(8,4),(2,1)}, each giving 2048+1024+512=3584 attended tokens/layer; L-RECENT recases budget as 2048+1536+0=3584. All InfLLM structural claims are paper-sourced general knowledge per researcher's accepted provenance labeling; nothing in this document is presented as an executed result.", "assumptions": ["Primary variant = OpenBMB tuning-free InfLLM (Zhang et al. 2024); Wang et al. trained-retriever variant out of scope for arms (researcher directive)", "One 7B-class YaRN-scaled model; 13B replication optional (researcher directive)", "Budget ~1-2k A100-80GB-hours; 3 seeds synthetic / 1 seed organic; single-seed organic results are descriptive only", "Task families: synthetic NIAH-style (oracle/recall defined) + localization-amenable LongBench subsets only; summarization/code excluded because oracle is undefined", "Official InfLLM/LongBench code obtainable externally; implementation audit vs. official defaults is a pre-execution gate that may reset anchor values", "Nominal anchor W=2048, B=128, n_recent=8, k=4 (paper-derived general knowledge, subject to the audit gate)", "Feasibility figures are estimates from a stated arithmetic compute model (120 effective TFLOPS, linear-in-length prefill), not measurements", "This remains a pure design document: nothing was executed and no numbers herein are results"], "uncertainties": ["Official-config values (window/k/n_recent) vs. our assumed anchor — resolved only by post-hoc code audit before any real run", "True per-arm runtime; our estimate could be off by 2x either way; cut-first list bounds the exposure", "Annotation feasibility for the optional organic oracle subset (κ>=0.7 gate); flagged highest-risk, drops first", "Unidentifiable key×read-out and key×position interactions — accepted limitation, only a larger factorial could resolve", "Power for sub-5-point effects on primary contrasts; pre-committed to labeling such nulls as underpowered"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b480784cb881da41", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-b480784cb881da41", "responses_item_id": 